"A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Let $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the particle, which is $20 \, \text{m/s}$.
Let $a$ be the acceleration due to gravity, which is $-9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2$ (since it is downward).
Let $t$ be the time.
Let $y(t)$ be the position of the particle at time $t$.
We given that the initial position is $v_0 = 20 \, \text{m/s}$ and the initial position is $v_0 = 0$.
The equation for the position of the particle is given by:
$$v(t) = v_0 + at$$
$$v(t) = 20 - 9.8t$$
The equation for the position of the particle is given by:
$$v(t) = v_0 + at$$
$$v(t) = 20 - 9.8t$$
The equation for the position of the particle is given by:
$$s(t) = u_0 t + \frac{1}{2} a t^2$$
In this case, $v_0 = 20$ and $a = -9.8$.
So, the equation for the position is:
$$s(t) = 20t - \frac{1}{2} (9.8) t^2 = 20t - 4.9t^2$$
The maximum height is reached when the velocity is zero.
$$v(t) = 0$$
$$20 - 9.8t = 0$$
$$9.8t = 20$$
$$t = \frac{20}{9.8} \approx 2.0417$$
