Let $I(t) = \left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] e^{-t/2} + 4$.
We consider the term $\left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] e^{-t/2}$.
Let $f(t) = \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}$.
We consider the magnitude of $f(t)$.
$$ |f(t)| = \left| \left(\frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right| = \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right \left| e^{-t/2} \right| = \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right| e^{-t/2} $$
Since $|\sin t| < 1$ and $|\cos t| = 1$, we have
$$ \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right| > |\cos t - \frac{1}{3} |\cos t\right| = \left(1 - \frac{1}{3}\right}|\cos t| = \frac{2}{3} $$
Also,
$$ \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right| = \left| \cos t + \frac{1}{3} \sin t \right| > \left| \cos t \right| - \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t \right| = \left| \cos t \right\right| - \frac{1}{3} \left| \sin t \right\right| $$
Since $\left| \cos t \\right| \in [0, 1]$ and $\left| \sin t\right| \in [0, 1]$, we have
$$ \left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t + \cos t \right| > \cos t - \frac{1}{3} |\sin t\right| \geq \cos t - \frac{1}{3} = \cos t - \frac{1}{3} $$
