$\cos(180^{\circ} + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$
$\sin(\alpha + 360^{\circ}) = \sin \alpha$
$\sin(-\alpha - 180^{\circ}) = \sin(-(\alpha + 180^{\circ})) = -\sin(\alpha + 180^{\circ}) = -(-\sin \alpha) = \sin \alpha$
$\cos(-180^{\circ} - \alpha) = \cos(-(\alpha + 180^{\circ})) = \cos(\alpha + 180^{\circ}) = -\cos \alpha$
So the expression is
$$\frac{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot (\sin \alpha)}{(\sin \alpha) \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \frac{-(\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha)}{-(\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha)} = 1$$

