","(a) $\int_{-1}^{1} 2^{x} d x$
We know that $\int a^x d x = \frac{a^x}{\ln a} + C$.
So, $\int 2^x d x = \frac{2^x}{\ln 2} + C$.
Then,
$$ \int_{-1}^{1} 2^{x} d x = \left[ \frac{2^x}{\ln 2} \right]_{-1}^{1} = \frac{2^1}{\ln 2} - \frac{2^{-1}}{\ln 2} = \frac{2}{\ln 2} - \frac{1}{2 \ln 2} = \frac{4-1}{4 \ln 2} = \frac{3}{4 \ln 2} $$

(b) $\int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x$
We have
$$ \int (3 e^x + 2 \sqrt{x}) d x = 3 \int e^x d x + 2 \int \sqrt{x} d x = 3 e^x + 2 \int x^{1/2} d x = 3 e^x + 2 \left( \frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2} \right) + C = 3 e^x + \frac{4}{3} x^{3/2} + C $$
Thus,
$$ \int_{0}^{1}\left(3 e^{x}+2 \sqrt{x}\right) d x = \left[ 3 e^x + \frac{4}{3} x^{3/2} \right]_0^1 = \left( 3 e^1 + \frac{4}{3} (1)^{3/2} \right) - \left( 3 e^0 + \frac{4}{3} (0)^{3/2} \right) = \left( 3 e + \frac{4}{3} \right) - (3 + 0) = 3 e + \frac{4}{3} - 3 = 3 e - \frac{5}{3} $$

