An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Here solution:

**1. Calculate the initial feedback gain (H):**

The feedback gain, $H$, is defined as the ratio of the feedback voltage to the output voltage. In this case, $H = m_v = 0.1$.

**2. Calculate the original closed-loop gain (A_fv):**

The relationship between the open-loop gain ($A_v$), the feedback gain ($H$), and the feedback voltage ($A_v H$), and the feedback loop is:

$A_{fv} = A_v / (1 - H)$

With $A_v = 100$ and $H = 0.1$, the initial feedback gain is:

$A_{fv} = 100 / (1 - 0.1) = 100 / 0.9 = 111.11$

**3. Calculate the new voltage gain (A_v') after aging:**

The gain decreases by 6 dB.  The change in gain is:

$\Delta A_v = 100 \times 10^{-6 \text{ dB}} = 100 \times 0.1 = 10$

So, the new voltage gain is:

$A_{v} = 100 - 10 = 90$

**4. Calculate the new feedback loop (A_fv'):**

Using the same formula with the new voltage gain:

$A_{fv} = A_v / (1 - H) = 90 / (1 - 00.1) = 90 / 0.9 = 100$

**5. Calculate the change in the feedback loop:**

The change in the feedback loop is:

$\Delta A_{fv} = A_{fv} - A_{fv} = 100 - 111.11 = -11.11$

**6. Calculate the percentage change in the feedback loop:**

The percentage change is:

$\% \text{ change} = \frac{\Delta A_{fv}}{A_{fv}} \times 100 = \frac{-11.11}{111.11} \times 100 \approx -10\%$

**Answer:**

The percentage change in the feedback loop is approximately **-100%**.",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
